WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.160 --> 00:00:07.560
Atherosclerosis is a life threatening disease that may have begun to develop during childhood

00:00:08.040 --> 00:00:15.800
This condition is a process in which deposits of fatty material called plaque, build up inside the walls of arteries

00:00:15.800 --> 00:00:19.580
reducing or completely blocking blood flow.

00:00:19.580 --> 00:00:24.140
In extreme cases, this disease can lead to a heart attack or a stroke.

00:00:25.420 --> 00:00:37.200
Risk factors for atherosclerosis include high blood pressure, high cholesterol, tobacco smoke, diabetes, obesity, and physical inactivity.

00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:42.500
Although the exact causes of atherosclerosis are not clear

00:00:42.620 --> 00:00:49.020
many scientists think it begins with damage to the inner wall of an artery called the endothelium

00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:52.060
over time, substances traveling in the blood

00:00:52.060 --> 00:01:00.020
such as cholesterol, fats, and cellular wastes products accumulate inside the damage area of the arterial wall

00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:07.200
chemical reactions occurring within the build up of material cause cholesterol molecules to oxidize

00:01:07.900 --> 00:01:14.900
This initiates an inflammatory response in which the endothelial cells at the damaged site release chemicals

00:01:14.900 --> 00:01:16.660
that signal a call for help

00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:22.880
In response, monocytes from the bloodstream travel to the damaged site

00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:34.480
stimulation from oxidized cholesterol converts the monocytes into macrophages

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:39.680
the macrophages eat and digest the cholesterol molecules

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:45.520
as a result of this process, the macrophages change into foam cells

00:01:46.300 --> 00:01:48.460
which accumulate to form plaque

00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:54.560
As the plaque increases in size, the arterial wall thickens and hardens.

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:08.200
At the same time, smooth muscle cells within the arterial wall begin to multiply.

00:02:11.340 --> 00:02:16.060
Then, most of the smooth muscle cells move to the surface of the plaque.

00:02:32.220 --> 00:02:37.900
These cells contribute to the formation of a firm, fibrous cap, covering the plaque.

00:02:48.080 --> 00:02:53.680
Over time, the cap may erode and break open, releasing plaque into the bloodstream.

00:02:55.040 --> 00:03:00.240
The plaque can flow downstream and contribute to the formation of a blood clot

00:03:00.240 --> 00:03:02.520
which can stop blood flow.

00:03:02.520 --> 00:03:08.840
As a result, limited blood supply is available to the area surrounding the partially blocked artery

00:03:08.840 --> 00:03:12.520
degrading and potentially killing the neighboring tissue

00:03:12.660 --> 00:03:18.820
Significant damage in organs such as the heart or brain can result in a heart attack or stroke

